1
00:00:01,508 --> 00:00:08,055
In 1739, a foreign king walked into
Delhi, stayed 57 days, and left with so

2
00:00:08,058 --> 00:00:12,856
much treasure that he canceled taxes back
home in Persia for three whole years.

3
00:00:13,479 --> 00:00:18,499
Not the British, not a Mongol, a
guy most people have never heard of.

4
00:00:19,431 --> 00:00:22,799
And here's the thing, that wasn't
even India's worst century.

5
00:00:23,474 --> 00:00:27,276
For most of the last thousand years,
India was the richest place on Earth.

6
00:00:27,765 --> 00:00:31,412
At one point, this one country
produced about a quarter of

7
00:00:31,474 --> 00:00:33,250
everything made on the entire planet.

8
00:00:33,870 --> 00:00:37,102
Two hundred years later,
it produced around 2%.

9
00:00:37,974 --> 00:00:43,340
It was invaded, looted, split in half,
and starved, and it is now the most

10
00:00:43,342 --> 00:00:46,741
populated country in the world and one
of the fastest-growing economies on it.

11
00:00:47,345 --> 00:00:52,478
So how does a place get robbed that
many times and keep standing back up?

12
00:00:52,913 --> 00:00:56,246
To understand that, we have to
go back to a temple on the coast

13
00:00:56,260 --> 00:00:58,782
that kept getting emptied out

14
00:01:02,441 --> 00:01:05,547
Around the year 1000,
India was not a country.

15
00:01:05,850 --> 00:01:08,841
Let me be clear about that because
everything else depends on it.

16
00:01:09,550 --> 00:01:10,150
Look at this map.

17
00:01:10,697 --> 00:01:12,061
See all those separate colors?

18
00:01:12,650 --> 00:01:17,093
Hundred of kingdoms, dozens of languages,
and a warrior class called Rajputs

19
00:01:17,409 --> 00:01:20,881
who were generally some of the bravest
fighters in the world and absolutely

20
00:01:20,883 --> 00:01:22,265
could not stop fighting each other.

21
00:01:23,018 --> 00:01:25,082
Rajputs had a code of honor.

22
00:01:25,346 --> 00:01:27,285
You fought fair, give warning.

23
00:01:27,730 --> 00:01:30,531
You didn't hit a man who'd already lost.

24
00:01:30,606 --> 00:01:34,815
It was beautiful and was about to get
very expensive for them because up

25
00:01:34,815 --> 00:01:40,698
in Afghanistan, a ruler named Mahmud
Afghanis had noticed something different.

26
00:01:41,186 --> 00:01:45,261
India's temples weren't just a
place of worship, they were banks.

27
00:01:45,725 --> 00:01:51,630
Kings donated gold to them for
centuries, solid gold statues, jewelry,

28
00:01:51,660 --> 00:01:54,626
doors, vaults nobody had ever touched.

29
00:01:54,858 --> 00:02:00,163
So Mahmud came down through the mountain
passes and robbed them, and then he

30
00:02:00,253 --> 00:02:06,911
did it again, and again, and again, 17
separate raids over 30 years of a time.

31
00:02:08,122 --> 00:02:13,723
His most famous target was the temple
of Somnath on the western coast in 1025.

32
00:02:14,328 --> 00:02:19,021
And the gold he carried out of that
single building funded an em- entire

33
00:02:19,131 --> 00:02:21,480
empire back in, in Afghanistan.

34
00:02:21,834 --> 00:02:23,616
He never tried to stay.

35
00:02:23,954 --> 00:02:26,683
He just came, emptied
the place and went home.

36
00:02:27,046 --> 00:02:31,957
17 times, nobody stopped him 'cause
there was nobody to stop him.

37
00:02:33,194 --> 00:02:35,921
There were just a few hundred
kings who all assumed that it

38
00:02:35,921 --> 00:02:37,300
was somebody else's problem.

39
00:02:38,149 --> 00:02:42,902
So about 170 years later, a
different Afghan ruler showed

40
00:02:42,906 --> 00:02:44,007
up with a different plan.

41
00:02:44,070 --> 00:02:49,120
His name was Muhammad Ghori, and he
did not want gold, he wanted the land.

42
00:02:50,015 --> 00:02:54,652
He ran into the strongest king in the
Northern India, Prithviraj Chauhan,

43
00:02:55,673 --> 00:03:00,031
at a place called Tarain in 1191.

44
00:03:00,786 --> 00:03:03,321
Prithviraj crushed him completely.

45
00:03:03,857 --> 00:03:06,186
Ghori was captured alive
on the battlefield.

46
00:03:06,541 --> 00:03:10,466
Then, according to the stories,
Prithviraj did the honorable thing.

47
00:03:11,656 --> 00:03:15,305
"Sir, we have captured
the invader." "Excellent.

48
00:03:16,106 --> 00:03:23,672
Bring him here." "He's right on his
knees, weeping and actually-" "Right.

49
00:03:23,870 --> 00:03:27,793
Let him go." "I'm sorry?" "He's beaten.

50
00:03:28,757 --> 00:03:30,993
It's dishonorable to kill a beaten man

51
00:03:33,822 --> 00:03:37,049
came specifically to take
our kingdom, and he failed.

52
00:03:37,815 --> 00:03:38,762
Give him a horse.

53
00:03:39,206 --> 00:03:40,010
A horse?

54
00:03:40,891 --> 00:03:42,980
He cannot walk back to Afghanistan.

55
00:03:44,054 --> 00:03:47,421
Sir, what if he comes, comes back?

56
00:03:48,095 --> 00:03:49,432
Then I'll beat him again.

57
00:03:50,147 --> 00:03:52,105
Should I write that down somewhere?

58
00:03:53,161 --> 00:03:57,651
He came back, and one year later,
same field, and this time Ghori

59
00:03:57,668 --> 00:04:02,904
attacked at dawn before the Rajput
army had formed up, which was not

60
00:04:03,099 --> 00:04:04,434
how it was supposed to be done.

61
00:04:04,815 --> 00:04:09,152
Prithviraj lost, and the
Northern India lost with him.

62
00:04:09,882 --> 00:04:13,791
Ghori went home to Afghanistan and
handed this new Indian territory

63
00:04:14,115 --> 00:04:16,000
to a trusted commander to run.

64
00:04:16,427 --> 00:04:18,480
That commander was not a prince.

65
00:04:18,841 --> 00:04:19,712
He was not a noble.

66
00:04:20,254 --> 00:04:23,575
He had been bought and sold as property

67
00:04:27,132 --> 00:04:29,852
So in other words, he was a slave.

68
00:04:30,325 --> 00:04:36,391
So the slave name was Qutb-ud-Din Aibek,
and he had been a slave his whole life.

69
00:04:36,680 --> 00:04:39,689
So now that needs explaining
because slave in the system doesn't

70
00:04:39,689 --> 00:04:40,834
mean that you are picturing.

71
00:04:41,226 --> 00:04:45,303
And Central Asian rulers deliberately
bought boys, raised them, trained them

72
00:04:45,304 --> 00:04:48,570
as elite soldiers and administrators,
and promoted them on their merit.

73
00:04:48,876 --> 00:04:52,151
So the whole point was that a slave
had no powerful family behind him,

74
00:04:52,444 --> 00:04:54,303
so he'd owe everything to you.

75
00:04:54,385 --> 00:04:56,118
It was like an insurance policy.

76
00:04:56,403 --> 00:04:58,334
So yeah, the insurance policy took over.

77
00:04:58,393 --> 00:05:02,390
In two hundred and six when Ghori
was assassinated, Aibek simply

78
00:05:02,390 --> 00:05:04,337
declared himself Sultan of Delhi.

79
00:05:04,677 --> 00:05:10,576
And a man who had been sold for
money now was running Northern India.

80
00:05:10,812 --> 00:05:14,683
And to make sure nobody forget it, he
started building a victory tower in Delhi.

81
00:05:15,041 --> 00:05:16,554
It's called Qutub Minar.

82
00:05:16,745 --> 00:05:20,690
It's seventy-three meters of red
sandstone, and it's still standing

83
00:05:20,750 --> 00:05:25,224
today in present days, and it still
is the tallest brick minaret on Earth.

84
00:05:25,517 --> 00:05:28,164
And he didn't live to see it
finished, but in two hundred

85
00:05:28,168 --> 00:05:29,731
and ten, he died playing polo.

86
00:05:30,154 --> 00:05:32,570
So he fell off his horse
and his saddle through him.

87
00:05:32,840 --> 00:05:37,054
One of the most powerful men in
Asia was taken out by the sport.

88
00:05:37,366 --> 00:05:42,576
And what he left behind is called the
Delhi Sultanate, and it would run Northern

89
00:05:42,589 --> 00:05:44,639
India for over three hundred years.

90
00:05:44,979 --> 00:05:48,211
And here is the detail I
generally didn't expect to find.

91
00:05:48,260 --> 00:05:52,258
In twelve hundred and thirty-six,
the throne went to a woman.

92
00:05:52,523 --> 00:05:53,866
Her name was Raziya.

93
00:05:53,964 --> 00:05:57,895
Her father, the sultan, had four sons
and looked all of them and concluded

94
00:05:57,900 --> 00:06:03,268
out loud that his daughter was better
than the lot, so he named her his heir.

95
00:06:03,887 --> 00:06:05,195
The nobles hated it.

96
00:06:05,387 --> 00:06:09,248
Raziya ignored them, dropped the
veil, wore a tunic, rode at the

97
00:06:09,263 --> 00:06:14,781
front of her own army, and ran
the sultanate for over four years.

98
00:06:14,962 --> 00:06:20,486
Four years later, and then the nobles
who had never accepted her finally got

99
00:06:20,486 --> 00:06:24,306
over it, and she was overthrown and
killed in twelve hundred and forty.

100
00:06:25,060 --> 00:06:28,513
It would be almost seven hundred
years before another woman ran

101
00:06:28,538 --> 00:06:30,148
anything in India at that level.

102
00:06:30,601 --> 00:06:35,424
The sultanate survived her, but it
was about to face the single most

103
00:06:35,433 --> 00:06:40,014
frightening thing in the medieval
world, arriving from the northwest

104
00:06:40,014 --> 00:06:42,419
with a hundred and thousands of horses

105
00:06:44,855 --> 00:06:46,761
The Mongols, they were coming.

106
00:06:47,260 --> 00:06:50,765
They had already flattened
Baghdad, Persia, and most of China.

107
00:06:51,241 --> 00:06:55,500
When they turned toward India,
everyone assumed Delhi was finished.

108
00:06:56,006 --> 00:07:00,868
The man sitting on the throne was Alauddin
Khalji, and he was ruthless in a very

109
00:07:00,868 --> 00:07:02,872
specific and a very effective way.

110
00:07:03,264 --> 00:07:07,503
He took the throne by inviting his
own uncle, who was also the sultan,

111
00:07:07,672 --> 00:07:10,688
to a friendly riverside meeting
and having him beheaded there.

112
00:07:11,285 --> 00:07:15,218
Family occasions in this dynasty
were tense, but the Mongols found

113
00:07:15,258 --> 00:07:17,082
him very extremely annoying.

114
00:07:18,103 --> 00:07:23,522
Between roughly 1297 and 1308,
they invaded again and again.

115
00:07:24,150 --> 00:07:27,759
Once they got all the way to Delhi
itself and camped outside the wall.

116
00:07:28,187 --> 00:07:30,977
Alauddin beat them back every single time.

117
00:07:31,606 --> 00:07:35,980
He built forts along the invasion
routes, kept a permanent standing

118
00:07:36,001 --> 00:07:40,863
army instead of calling up soldiers
at harvest time, and India became one

119
00:07:40,863 --> 00:07:45,807
of the very few places the Mongols
tried repeatedly and never took.

120
00:07:46,555 --> 00:07:50,661
But to keep a permanent enemy,
you have to pay a permanent army,

121
00:07:50,948 --> 00:07:54,556
and the soldiers need to eat, and
Alauddin couldn't afford both.

122
00:07:54,775 --> 00:07:59,118
So he invented something that the world
had basically never seen at this scale.

123
00:07:59,311 --> 00:08:01,556
He fixed the price of everything.

124
00:08:02,101 --> 00:08:04,127
Not suggested, fixed.

125
00:08:04,423 --> 00:08:10,612
Grain, cloth, horses, cattle, sugar,
vegetables, slave, everything got

126
00:08:10,612 --> 00:08:12,677
an official prices set by the state.

127
00:08:14,103 --> 00:08:18,198
Then he built an entire inspection
bureaucracy to enforce it, with

128
00:08:18,234 --> 00:08:22,640
government-run markets and undercover
agents sent to buy things and

129
00:08:23,175 --> 00:08:25,507
check nobody was overcharging.

130
00:08:26,695 --> 00:08:27,289
"Right.

131
00:08:27,618 --> 00:08:32,453
Wheat is now this price." "What if the
harvest is bad?" "Still that price."

132
00:08:32,823 --> 00:08:37,418
"What if the harvest is amazing?"
"Still that same price." "Sir,

133
00:08:37,693 --> 00:08:39,652
that's not how really market works."

134
00:08:40,093 --> 00:08:42,224
"I know exactly how market works.

135
00:08:42,757 --> 00:08:47,384
That's the problem." "What if
merchant cheats?" "Then I'll take

136
00:08:47,384 --> 00:08:53,036
him exactly what he overcharged."
"Money?" "Flesh, same weight.

137
00:08:53,689 --> 00:08:55,574
I'll go and tell the merchant.

138
00:08:55,883 --> 00:08:59,214
Tell them warmly." Well, it worked.

139
00:08:59,614 --> 00:09:04,444
Prices in Delhi stayed flat for
years, and his whole army got paid

140
00:09:04,444 --> 00:09:08,015
on a small salary because that
salary actually bought things.

141
00:09:08,367 --> 00:09:13,060
It is one of the most aggressive economic
experiments on the entire medieval world,

142
00:09:13,082 --> 00:09:16,902
and it happened in Delhi in the year 1300.

143
00:09:17,045 --> 00:09:21,553
Alauddin died in 1316, and within a
couple of decades, the throne went

144
00:09:21,553 --> 00:09:26,309
to a man who was, by every account,
brilliant, genuinely learned,

145
00:09:26,470 --> 00:09:31,453
spoke multiple languages, studied
mathematics, philosophy, and medicine.

146
00:09:31,911 --> 00:09:36,879
He was also about to have two ideas
so catastrophically wrong that

147
00:09:36,892 --> 00:09:40,838
they are still taught in India
today in schools as a warning

148
00:09:44,801 --> 00:09:48,804
His name was Muhammad bin Tughlaq,
and idea number one was that

149
00:09:48,816 --> 00:09:50,137
Delhi was in the wrong place.

150
00:09:50,415 --> 00:09:51,547
He had a point, honestly.

151
00:09:52,015 --> 00:09:55,995
The empire had expanded south, and
Delhi was stuck up in the far north,

152
00:09:56,780 --> 00:09:59,645
weeks of travel from his half territory.

153
00:10:00,849 --> 00:10:03,095
A capital in the middle
would be easier to run.

154
00:10:03,555 --> 00:10:04,225
Reasonable.

155
00:10:04,801 --> 00:10:08,419
His solution was to move the capital
roughly eleven hundred kilometers south to

156
00:10:08,420 --> 00:10:11,257
a city re-- and he renamed it Daulatabad.

157
00:10:11,773 --> 00:10:14,653
And when he said he moved
the capital city, he did not

158
00:10:14,669 --> 00:10:15,856
mean to move the government.

159
00:10:16,354 --> 00:10:20,609
He meant move the city, the nobles,
the clerks, the poets, the shopkeepers,

160
00:10:20,629 --> 00:10:24,172
ordinary families, everybody
across the country in the heat.

161
00:10:24,752 --> 00:10:27,926
So enormous number of people died
on the road, and when the survivors

162
00:10:27,952 --> 00:10:32,419
arrived and settled in, he looked at
the situation, realized Daulatabad was

163
00:10:32,422 --> 00:10:36,570
too far from the northern frontier to
defend against invasions and ordered

164
00:10:36,614 --> 00:10:42,247
everyone to march back to Delhi,
both directions on foot for nothing.

165
00:10:42,669 --> 00:10:43,982
Idea number two was money.

166
00:10:44,230 --> 00:10:48,929
Silver was running short worldwide, so
he decided to issue token currency, brass

167
00:10:49,003 --> 00:10:53,520
and copper coins that were officially
worth the same as silver ones, which

168
00:10:53,520 --> 00:10:55,766
is actually how modern money works.

169
00:10:55,780 --> 00:10:57,898
So in principle, ahead of his time.

170
00:10:58,568 --> 00:11:01,389
But in practice, he
forgot one small thing.

171
00:11:02,902 --> 00:11:05,299
The coin are a huge success, Your Majesty.

172
00:11:05,314 --> 00:11:06,733
Circulation is enormous.

173
00:11:07,123 --> 00:11:07,788
Wonderful.

174
00:11:07,841 --> 00:11:09,354
The treasury must be full.

175
00:11:09,536 --> 00:11:10,330
Full of coins.

176
00:11:10,392 --> 00:11:11,074
Excellent.

177
00:11:11,507 --> 00:11:12,614
Not once we made.

178
00:11:12,675 --> 00:11:13,163
Sorry?

179
00:11:13,647 --> 00:11:17,459
Every household with a furnace is
making them, sir. My neighbor heard

180
00:11:17,459 --> 00:11:19,098
the forty-nine, and he's a baker.

181
00:11:19,169 --> 00:11:19,893
Can they do it?

182
00:11:19,989 --> 00:11:21,646
It's a brass, sir. It's a pan.

183
00:11:21,971 --> 00:11:24,795
And they are paying their taxes
with this enthusiastically.

184
00:11:24,884 --> 00:11:26,084
How enthusiastically?

185
00:11:26,386 --> 00:11:30,355
We have had paid him four more times
this year by a man who owns a goat.

186
00:11:31,432 --> 00:11:34,144
He had to cancel the whole scheme
and buy the fake coins back with

187
00:11:34,154 --> 00:11:38,833
the silver, and which empire
the treasury was trying to fill.

188
00:11:38,849 --> 00:11:43,975
The historians, Ibn Battuta, a Moroccan
traveler who actually worked as a judge

189
00:11:43,993 --> 00:11:46,933
in Tughlaq court, wrote all of it down.

190
00:11:47,715 --> 00:11:51,760
And his account is one of the greatest
eyewitness document of medieval world.

191
00:11:52,088 --> 00:11:52,788
So quick pause.

192
00:11:53,127 --> 00:11:57,137
If you're enjoying this, a like
can genuinely help us grow,

193
00:11:57,558 --> 00:11:59,637
and unlike the coin, it's free.

194
00:12:00,108 --> 00:12:03,992
And stay with me because
everything so far has been invaders

195
00:12:04,008 --> 00:12:05,468
taking the gold and going home.

196
00:12:05,640 --> 00:12:08,985
In about fifteen minutes, a trading
company in London with the shareholders

197
00:12:09,484 --> 00:12:13,670
and a quarterly dividend is going to
walk in and buy this entire country with

198
00:12:13,670 --> 00:12:19,286
a bribe, and they do something no one
invaders in thousand years had managed.

199
00:12:19,691 --> 00:12:22,915
So the sultanate were never recovered.

200
00:12:23,712 --> 00:12:27,080
Provinces broke away one after
another, and down in the south, in

201
00:12:27,081 --> 00:12:30,801
the middle of the chaos, two brothers
founded a city that would become,

202
00:12:31,073 --> 00:12:35,380
for a while, the richest place in
any Europe had ever laid eyes on.

203
00:12:37,215 --> 00:12:39,090
The city of diamonds sold in the street.

204
00:12:39,478 --> 00:12:44,783
The empires were called Vijayanagara,
founded in 1336, and its capital sat

205
00:12:44,783 --> 00:12:49,817
among enormous boulders filled in
southern India at a place called Hampi.

206
00:12:50,845 --> 00:12:53,670
I want to give you the description
from people who actually went there

207
00:12:53,752 --> 00:12:55,476
because they don't sound real.

208
00:12:56,045 --> 00:13:01,188
A Persian ambassador, Abdul Razak,
arrived in 1440s, reported that he has

209
00:13:01,189 --> 00:13:03,540
seen nothing like it anywhere on Earth.

210
00:13:03,590 --> 00:13:09,658
He describes bazaars where jewelers sold
pearls, rubbles, emeralds, and diamonds

211
00:13:09,661 --> 00:13:13,863
openly in the market, out on display,
the way they would sell vegetables.

212
00:13:14,874 --> 00:13:19,939
Later, a Portuguese visitor named
Domingo Paes described rose sellers

213
00:13:20,039 --> 00:13:25,064
at every corner of the city because
people there were-- simply couldn't

214
00:13:25,158 --> 00:13:27,009
get through a day without fresh roses.

215
00:13:27,242 --> 00:13:30,605
Fresh roses every single
day, a basic necessity.

216
00:13:31,501 --> 00:13:37,149
The empire ran on trade, exporting
cotton, spices, and iron, and importing

217
00:13:37,348 --> 00:13:39,867
war horses by the thousands from Arabia.

218
00:13:40,456 --> 00:13:45,871
At its height, the capital may held
several hundred thousand people, which

219
00:13:45,946 --> 00:13:50,593
would have made it one of the largest
cities in the world at the time, second

220
00:13:50,605 --> 00:13:52,726
only to Beijing by some estimates.

221
00:13:53,692 --> 00:13:57,516
It lasted for a bit over two
centuries, mostly by playing

222
00:13:57,610 --> 00:13:59,646
its neighbors off each other.

223
00:14:00,005 --> 00:14:03,369
The problem with playing off your
neighbor off each other is that eventually

224
00:14:03,369 --> 00:14:05,526
your neighbors have a conversation.

225
00:14:05,968 --> 00:14:12,271
So in 1956, uh, five neighboring
sultanates put aside their own

226
00:14:12,271 --> 00:14:17,198
rivalries, combined their armies,
and met Vijayanagara at Talikota.

227
00:14:17,673 --> 00:14:22,496
The empire commander was cut down,
his army broke, and victorious armies

228
00:14:22,506 --> 00:14:24,681
walked into undefended capital.

229
00:14:25,116 --> 00:14:26,466
They did not loot it for a day.

230
00:14:26,723 --> 00:14:31,060
They stayed there for months,
systematically dismantling one of

231
