381
00:24:15,946 --> 00:24:21,322
The company sent Robert Sylvie with
about 3,000 men to take it back.

382
00:24:21,783 --> 00:24:23,943
Siraj had around 50,000.

383
00:24:25,566 --> 00:24:27,156
Sylvie didn't beat the army.

384
00:24:27,329 --> 00:24:28,121
He bought it.

385
00:24:28,681 --> 00:24:31,783
Their commander, his name is Mir Jafar.

386
00:24:32,390 --> 00:24:36,559
He commands roughly a third of their
forces and he's happy in his work?

387
00:24:36,889 --> 00:24:37,459
He's not.

388
00:24:37,912 --> 00:24:39,409
He would rather be ruler himself.

389
00:24:40,742 --> 00:24:43,471
How much does rather cost, sir?

390
00:24:43,745 --> 00:24:45,049
We have 3,000 men.

391
00:24:45,243 --> 00:24:45,908
They have 50.

392
00:24:46,332 --> 00:24:49,388
Which is why I'm asking about the
price rather than the tactics.

393
00:24:50,009 --> 00:24:55,657
And if he takes the money and fights
anyway, then why are we-- then they're--

394
00:24:55,676 --> 00:24:58,999
why-- then we are in the exactly
same situation we are already in.

395
00:24:59,890 --> 00:25:01,394
That's criminally logical.

396
00:25:01,635 --> 00:25:02,256
Write to him.

397
00:25:02,524 --> 00:25:03,206
Be generous.

398
00:25:03,347 --> 00:25:04,168
Mention the throne.

399
00:25:04,960 --> 00:25:08,564
Do we intently-- Do we intend
to actually give him the throne?

400
00:25:09,045 --> 00:25:15,253
We intend to give him the chair At
Pelsey, Mir Jafar's section of the

401
00:25:15,253 --> 00:25:17,905
army simply stood there and watched.

402
00:25:18,082 --> 00:25:19,557
Sirawsan's force collapsed.

403
00:25:19,836 --> 00:25:24,532
Mir Jafar got the throne and the company
